    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d385">

                <pb facs="0523" n="265" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg265"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/1244</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at London</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Skinner</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-06-12">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 12, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 10.30 a. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Department’s 11th. Goods originating Belgium are presumptively enemy origin and
                    it lies upon those interested to prove that Germany would not profit from
                    intended operation. In practice this is difficult to prove, as Belgian
                    representatives not in position to ascertain material facts. Am assured each
                    case will be considered upon merits and whenever it can be demonstrated that
                    transaction is for benefit Belgians, to exclusion of German advantage permission
                    will be granted.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Skinner</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
